\n
\nTables I and II report the main comparison on CoIN-6 and CoIN-Long-10 across sixteen baselines plus PRISM and three Dirichlet concentrations. PRISM holds the top rank on AA in every configuration and on BWT in all but one: Fed-PCLR [10]’s progressive rank compression edges ahead by 0.40.4 pp on LLaVA-1.5-7B at β=0.1\beta=0.1 (the most adverse non-IID setting). The gap inverts on the longer Long-10 sequence, where PRISM’s per-expert capacity outweighs Fed-PCLR’s compression advantage. At the headline cell (β=0.3\beta=0.3, C=5C=5), PRISM improves BWT by +4.47+4.47 pp on CoIN-6 over the monolithic gradient-subspace baseline Fed-KeepLoRA [25], consistent with the joint effect of C3–C5 absent from the monolithic baseline. Against the pure MoE router Fed-MoDE [9], AA rises +3.90+3.90 pp while forgetting falls 9.99.9 pp, confirming that routing-weighted gradient projection (C1–C4 jointly) outperforms routing-only specialization. Against the strongest federated multimodal baseline Fed-Duet [11], AA margins widen from +3.23+3.23 pp on CoIN-6 to +6.06+6.06 pp on Long-10, consistent with orthogonality-preserving aggregation (C5) accumulating larger advantages over longer sequences.


