\nTable III confirms that each design component contributes substantively (numbers below as 7​B/13​B7\text{B}/13\text{B}). Removing PE-FOSU (replacing federated basis aggregation with per-client independent bases) causes the sharpest AA drop (−4.66/−4.28-4.66/-4.28 pp), which confirms C5’s necessity under FedAvg. Removing the per-expert basis collapses PRISM to a Fed-KeepLoRA-style monolithic protection and drops AA by −3.77/−3.96-3.77/-3.96 pp with Δ\DeltaBWT =−3.18/−3.68=-3.18/-3.68 pp, in line with the per-expert granularity argument of C3. Unfreezing the router restores routing drift and costs −4.19/−3.78-4.19/-3.78 pp AA with −2.97/−3.50-2.97/-3.50 pp BWT, in line with the routing-stability evidence reported in Supp B. Interference-informed scheduling contributes the smallest but still non-trivial −1.83/−1.79-1.83/-1.79 pp; its role is to reallocate a fixed budget rather than add new capacity.

